The Security Council,
Recalling its resolutions 2014 (2011), 2051 (2012), 2140 (2014), 2216 (2015), 2266 (2016), 2342 (2017), 2456 (2019), 2511 (2020), 2564 (2021), 2624 (2022), and its subsequent relevant resolutions and presidential statements concerning Yemen,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the unity, sovereignty, independence and territorial integrity of Yemen, and its support for the Yemeni people,
Recalling the Gulf Cooperation Council Initiative and Implementation Mechanism, the outcomes of the comprehensive National Dialogue Conference, and the Riyadh Agreement, and reaffirming that the only viable path forward is an inclusive Yemeni-led and Yemeni-owned political process under United Nations auspices,
Taking note of the final report of the Panel of Experts on Yemen established pursuant to resolution 2140 (2014) (S/2025/650), and the findings contained therein, including continued violations of the targeted arms embargo and the threat posed by illicit transfers of weapons and components,
Expressing grave concern at the continued threat to maritime security in the Red Sea, Gulf of Aden and Bab al-Mandab, including attacks on commercial and civilian vessels, the seizure and detention of vessels and crews, and the impact on navigation, regional stability and the delivery of humanitarian assistance,
Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks by the Houthis on commercial and civilian vessels, on civilians and civilian infrastructure in Yemen, and cross-border attacks, including against Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates and Israel, and demanding their immediate cessation,
Expressing grave concern at the humanitarian situation in Yemen, including food insecurity, displacement, the threat of famine, and obstacles to the delivery of humanitarian assistance, and stressing the need for unconditional, safe and unhindered humanitarian access to all civilians in need throughout Yemen,
Condemning violations of international humanitarian law and international human rights law, including sexual and gender-based violence, the recruitment and use of children, attacks on humanitarian and United Nations personnel, arbitrary detention, and restrictions on freedom of movement, and stressing the need for accountability,
Expressing concern at the continued presence and activity of AQAP and ISIL affiliates in Yemen and the threat they pose to regional peace and security,
Recalling its resolutions 1325 (2000), 2467 (2019) and subsequent resolutions on women, peace and security, and emphasizing the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in the political process,
Reaffirming the need for all parties to comply with their obligations under international law, including international humanitarian law and international human rights law,
Emphasizing that the measures imposed by resolutions 2140 (2014) and 2216 (2015) are not intended to have adverse humanitarian consequences for the civilian population of Yemen, nor to restrict civilian access to humanitarian assistance, commercial imports or remittances,
Taking note of the work of the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for Yemen and reaffirming support for a United Nations-led political process,
Determining that the situation in Yemen continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Reaffirms its commitment to the unity, sovereignty, independence and territorial integrity of Yemen, and to a peaceful, inclusive and Yemeni-led political settlement;
2. Demands that the Houthis immediately cease all attacks on commercial and civilian vessels in the Red Sea, Gulf of Aden and Bab al-Mandab, release all seized vessels and crews, and cease all cross-border attacks and attacks against civilians and civilian infrastructure;
3. Calls upon all parties to the conflict to cease hostilities, de-escalate, and engage constructively with the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for Yemen, without preconditions, in an inclusive political process that includes women, youth and civil society, and to uphold the Riyadh Agreement;
4. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of all United Nations personnel, humanitarian workers, and other detained civilians, including the crew of the MV Galaxy Leader and the MV Rawabee, and calls for the safe, dignified and voluntary return of internally displaced persons;
5. Condemns violations and abuses of international human rights law and violations of international humanitarian law, including sexual and gender-based violence, the recruitment and use of children in armed conflict, attacks on schools and hospitals, and denial of humanitarian access, and stresses the need to ensure accountability for such acts;
6. Urges all parties to facilitate unconditional, safe and unhindered humanitarian access to all civilians in need, including through all ports and across conflict lines, and to protect humanitarian personnel, facilities and assets, and to respect international humanitarian law;
7. Expresses serious concern at the findings of the Panel of Experts concerning continued violations of the targeted arms embargo, including the illicit transfer and diversion of weapons and components, and calls upon all Member States to fully implement the measures imposed by resolutions 2140 (2014) and 2216 (2015), and to report possible violations to the Committee established pursuant to resolution 2140 (2014);
8. Encourages regional cooperation at sea and overland to detect and prevent violations of the targeted arms embargo, in accordance with international law, and calls for enhanced efforts to counter maritime smuggling and the illicit financing of armed groups;
9. Reaffirms the need to address the threat posed by AQAP, ISIL (Da’esh) and all other associated individuals, groups, undertakings and entities, and calls upon all parties to refrain from actions that exacerbate instability or enable terrorist groups to exploit the conflict;
10. Reaffirms its support for the Special Envoy of the Secretary-General for Yemen and the United Nations-led political process, and calls upon all parties to cooperate fully with the United Nations and to ensure the safety and security of United Nations personnel and humanitarian workers;
11. Decides to renew until 15 November 2026 the measures imposed by paragraphs 11 and 15 of resolution 2140 (2014), reaffirms the provisions of paragraphs 12, 13, 14 and 16 of resolution 2140 (2014), and further reaffirms the provisions of paragraphs 14 to 17 of resolution 2216 (2015);
12. Decides to extend until 15 December 2026 the mandate of the Panel of Experts on Yemen established pursuant to resolution 2140 (2014), and requests the Panel to provide a midterm update to the Committee by 15 April 2026 and a final report to the Security Council by 15 October 2026, and to continue to report on implementation of the measures, including violations of the targeted arms embargo;
13. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide the Panel of Experts with the necessary resources and support, and calls upon all Member States, United Nations entities and other relevant actors to cooperate fully with the Panel;
14. Reaffirms that the measures renewed in paragraph 11 are not intended to have adverse humanitarian consequences for the civilian population of Yemen, nor to restrict civilian access to humanitarian assistance, commercial imports or remittances, and recalls the Committee’s mandate to consider requests for humanitarian exemptions and ensure timely decision-making;
15. Requests the Committee established pursuant to resolution 2140 (2014) to continue its work, including consideration of the Panel’s recommendations, and to report to the Security Council on its activities;
16. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

